## 1. Why the six-row cover can be lifted directly

The remaining all-singleton profile has

```text
eight rows:
    rank(P_8)=2,
    d_8=3,
    nine singleton changed blocks,
    replacement-functional quotient dimension 9.
```

The rank-two projective-column lemma gives at least two three-clean coordinates. Fix one of them, call it `i`, and delete it. The sharpened `k=6` reduction proves that the resulting seven-row state has

```text
rank(P_7)=2,
d_7=3,
singleton-functional rank 8,
```

and every one- and two-coordinate puncture of `P_7` still has rank two. Every further one-coordinate deletion is therefore a synchronized six-row state with

```text
rank(P_6)=2,
d_6=3,
singleton-functional rank 7,
```

and every five-row puncture has deficiency two or three and fills its corresponding replacement-functional quotient.

Hence any actual global Profile B state appears in the existing coverage-correct six-row audit after deleting the three-clean coordinate `i` and any second external row.

## 2. Exact rank-two lift enumeration

Suppose a realizable rank-two coefficient space `P_t` projects to a fixed rank-two parent `P_{t-1}` after deleting the last coordinate. The coefficient space is generated by local root-block constraint spaces. Since its rank is two, a basis can be chosen in exactly one of the following forms:

1. one rank-two local constraint space from one actual block state; or
2. one rank-one local constraint from each of two distinct actual blocks.

A single actual block has only one state, so two rank-one constraints from different states of the same block are not allowed. Empty states supply rank at most two, singleton states rank at most one, and pair/complete states supply no coefficient constraint.

This is the same basis argument already used by the established six-row extension engine. The new verifier applies it generically at seven and eight rows. It keeps only local spaces whose last-coordinate projection lies in the fixed parent and requires the resulting rank-two span to project exactly to that parent. Therefore every realizable rank-two lift is covered.

## 3. Necessary seven-row conditions

For each surviving six-row state, the verifier restores one no-root row and exhausts every rank-two seven-row lift. A lift is retained only if:

1. all seven one-coordinate punctures retain coefficient rank two;
2. all twenty-one two-coordinate punctures retain coefficient rank two;
3. the seven-row virtual deficiency is exactly three;
4. at least eight virtual-complete blocks admit a compatible singleton state;
5. the span of all compatible singleton replacement classes has exact extra rank eight; and
6. every one-coordinate six-row puncture has deficiency three, singleton capacity at least seven, and exact singleton-functional rank seven.

Conditions 1 and 2 encode that the still-missing eighth coordinate can be the chosen three-clean coordinate: after restoring that coordinate, deletion of it together with any further zero, one, or two seven-row coordinates preserves coefficient rank two.

The functional requirements are necessary because the descended actual singleton classes span each clean deletion quotient. Allowing every compatible singleton block is an overapproximation, so failure of the enlarged span condition rigorously excludes the lift.

## 4. Necessary eight-row conditions

For every retained clean seven-row lift, the verifier restores a second no-root row and exhausts every realizable rank-two eight-row lift. It requires:

1. every one-coordinate puncture of `P_8` has rank two;
2. the eight-row virtual deficiency is exactly three;
3. at least nine virtual-complete blocks admit a compatible singleton state; and
4. the span of all compatible singleton replacement classes has exact extra rank nine.

The fixed seven-row parent already has every one- and two-coordinate puncture rank-preserving, so the last restored coordinate is certified to be three-clean in the eight-row lift. Thus the search does not merely find an arbitrary rank-two eight-row extension; it reconstructs the clean deletion fan required by the structural theorem.

The audit still does not insist that one fixed set of nine compatible blocks is the actual global singleton set. It therefore searches a superset of genuine Profile B realizations. Consequently:

> if the full 20,451-orbit cover returns zero global lifts, Profile B is impossible.

A survivor is only a finite global candidate and must still pass the exact nine-block realization/consistency conditions.

## 5. Exactness of the new rank decisions

All coefficient-space row-space operations and lift generation use the repository's exact rational row-space machinery.

The seven- and eight-row relation rows are constructed by the same nullspace/local-correction formula used at five and six rows. The new singleton-functional span tests use exact Gaussian elimination over Python `Fraction`, not modular ranks. A candidate is also rejected if its full canonical relation row span exceeds the complete-block relation span, because the established containment theorem is necessary for the clean seven-row and global eight-row states.

The underlying six-row cover retains its existing `2^127-1` Hadamard-certified modular rank checks. The newly added seven- and eight-row decisions require no finite-field certificate.

The verifier source was syntax-compiled before commit. No full mathematical execution has completed yet.

